Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for this Next.js project.

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured @vercel/analytics following the latest official Vercel documentation from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` package using npm
- Updated `package.json` with the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the resolved dependency tree

### 2. Configuration
Modified `src/app/layout.tsx`:
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Added `<Analytics />` component inside the `<body>` tag after the `<Providers>{children}</Providers>` element
- This follows the Next.js App Router pattern as specified in the official documentation

## Implementation Details
The Analytics component was placed according to Vercel's recommended Next.js App Router implementation:
- Located in the root layout file (`src/app/layout.tsx`)
- Positioned at the end of the `<body>` tag to track all page views
- Uses the framework-specific import path `@vercel/analytics/next` for optimal Next.js integration

## Verification Steps Completed
✓ Linter check passed with no errors
✓ Build completed successfully with no TypeScript or compilation errors
✓ All existing functionality preserved
✓ Lock files properly updated

## Next Steps
To activate Web Analytics:
1. Deploy this application to Vercel
2. Navigate to the project's Analytics section in the Vercel dashboard
3. Click the "Enable" button to activate Web Analytics
4. After deployment, analytics data will start being collected automatically

## Files Modified
- `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `package-lock.json` - Updated dependency lock file
- `src/app/layout.tsx` - Added Analytics component and import
